---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

// Issue #1102: the support page App Store Connect requires (App
// Information → Support URL). In-app feedback leads; the email path and the
// FAQ follow. There is deliberately no contact form: the site collects
// nothing (#830's out-of-scope list).
//
// Two inputs are deliberately pending (issue #1100 — the owner picks the
// public support address): the email address itself and the committed
// response time. They are marked as pending below rather than invented;
// until they land, this page points at the contact address already
// published in PRIVACY.md. Do not point App Store Connect at this URL until
// that follow-up has merged.
//
// The FAQ's guide mentions (#1106) link into the how-to guides, which
// carry the step-by-step versions of these answers.

const privacyPolicyUrl = "https://github.com/wjdavis5/lunarlog/blob/main/PRIVACY.md";
const privacyContactUrl = `${privacyPolicyUrl}#11-contact-information`;
const deleteAccountUrl = "/delete-account";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Support"
  description="Get help with lunarlog: send feedback from inside the app, answers to common questions about sharing, deletion, exports, and where your data lives."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Support</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      The fastest way to reach us is from inside the app. This page also
      answers the questions we hear most, and explains how to reach us when
      you can't open the app.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Send feedback from the app</h2>
      <!-- cite: lib/ui/settings/settings_screen.dart ("Send feedback" tile; "Support history" tile; the signed-out "Contact support" fallback). -->
      <ol class="doc__steps">
        <li>
          Open lunarlog and go to <strong>More</strong> in the bottom bar to
          reach <strong>Settings</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tap <strong>Send feedback</strong>. Write what happened — you can
          choose a category, and attaching a screenshot or diagnostics is
          always your choice, never automatic.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Replies land in <strong>Settings → Support history</strong>, inside
        the app. Feedback needs a signed-in account; if you're signed out you
        will see <strong>Contact support</strong> in the same place instead.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Reach us by email</h2>
      <p>
        If you can't open the app — or the matter is account or deletion
        related — you can write to us instead. Tell us what you need; if it's
        about an account, include the email address the account uses and
        nothing else.
      </p>
      <aside class="note note--pending">
        <span class="note__label">Pending publication</span>
        <p>
          A dedicated support address, and the response time we commit to, are
          being finalized and will be published here. Until then, the contact
          address already listed in
          <a href={privacyContactUrl}>our privacy policy</a> works.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Common questions</h2>

      <h3>How do I delete my account?</h3>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7; the walkthrough lives on the deletion page (issue #1102). -->
      <p>
        From <strong>Settings → Account → Delete account</strong>, on every
        platform. It happens immediately, and the page explains exactly what
        is and isn't removed — see
        <a href={deleteAccountUrl}>Delete your account</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Who can see a profile I share?</h3>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Guardian Roles & Read Visibility" and "Guardian Notes Are Visible To The Child". -->
      <p>
        Every accepted guardian on a profile — whatever their role — can read
        what's logged on it, with one deliberate exception: a day note the
        profile's subject marked <strong>private</strong> is visible only to
        her. Roles decide who can log and manage, not a sliding scale of what
        they can read.
      </p>

      <h3>How do I invite my partner, or set a profile up for my daughter?</h3>
      <p>
        From the profile's guardian management, you can invite another adult
        to share a profile you track on — or create a profile for someone else
        and invite her to log it herself. Both directions are first-class, and
        the guides walk each one:
        <a href="/guides/household-setup/">Setting up a household</a> and
        <a href="/guides/inviting-someone/">Inviting someone</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Does lunarlog work without a network?</h3>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Export Your Data" (offline export) and the project's offline-first posture; AGENTS.md project overview. -->
      <p>
        Yes. Logging, history, and estimates all work offline, and what you
        log syncs to your family's devices when a connection returns. Export
        works offline too — it is built from the copy on your device.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I get my data out?</h3>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 (JSON/CSV/FHIR/PDF exports, built on-device). -->
      <p>
        Any time, from <strong>Settings → Your data</strong>: a JSON file of
        everything, two CSV spreadsheets, a FHIR summary for clinicians, and a
        one-page PDF. All of them are built on your device and handed to your
        share sheet — no account required.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I bring data in from Clue or my health app?</h3>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7/§4 and docs/ops/play-health-declaration.md (user-initiated
           Apple Health / Health Connect import, full history — it reads menstrual-flow
           records as far back as the health store has them, per issue #992, and never
           overwrites a hand-logged value); bulk Clue import (issue #167/#172). -->
      <p>
        Yes. You can import history from Clue, and import your
        menstrual-flow history from Apple Health or Health Connect whenever
        you choose, as far back as your health app has it. Imports never
        overwrite a value you logged by hand. It's user-initiated only;
        lunarlog never reads your health store in the background.
        <a href="/guides/moving-your-data/">Moving your data</a> walks
        through it.
      </p>

      <h3>Is my data encrypted?</h3>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §6 ("Cloud Storage Encryption (not end-to-end)", TLS in transit, device-level protection, RLS). Honest posture per #1105. -->
      <p>
        On your device, the database is protected by your device's own
        encryption and unlocked only by your biometric or passcode. In the
        cloud, data travels over TLS and is stored with standard at-rest
        encryption — but it is <strong>not end-to-end encrypted</strong>: we
        protect it with access control and infrastructure encryption rather
        than keys only you hold. The privacy policy states this plainly, and
        so do we.
      </p>

      <h3>Does the app show ads or track me?</h3>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §1/§9 (no advertising, tracking = false). -->
      <p>
        No ads, no trackers, no analytics on this site. The app's crash
        reporting is anonymized and scrubbed, and every optional network
        feature — sync, alerts, feedback — is one you turn on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="doc__footer-note">
      This site collects nothing — no forms, no analytics, no cookies. The
      answers above summarize
      <a href={privacyPolicyUrl}>our privacy policy</a>, which is the
      authoritative version.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
